Clear the high half of an unsigned int that x86-64 code reads from a whole
64-bit register: where it widens the value to 64 bits and where it converts
it to floating point. Widen the size of a VLA's element before it scales an
index, so that the 64-bit add of the pointer takes a 64-bit product.

On a 64-bit target gen_cast emits nothing for a cast of a 64-bit value to
unsigned int, which leaves the value's high half in its register, and
nothing for the widening of an unsigned int to 64 bits, which takes the
high half for zero, as it is after a 32-bit load or a 32-bit operation.
A value cast down in a register and widened again kept its high half:
(unsigned long)(unsigned int)x, and x == (unsigned int)x, which the
kernel's USER_LEN32 tests, came out as x and as true. The widening of an
unsigned int on x86-64 now clears the high half with movl r, r. The
registers RC_INT names are rax, rcx and rdx, which need no REX prefix.

gen_cvt_itof converts an unsigned int with the 64-bit cvtsi2sd or
cvtsi2ss, and to long double with fildll of the whole register pushed, so
(double)(unsigned)(c ? a : 0) with a = 0x100000001 came out 4294967297.
It now clears the high half with movl r, r before either.

The size of a VLA's element is an int, so an index into a pointer to one
was scaled by a 32-bit imul, whose product the 64-bit add of the pointer
took as zero-extended: p + i with i = -1 moved p almost 4 GiB forward. The
size is now cast to long long first, which widens the index with it, by
movslq or movl, and makes the multiply 64 bits wide.

Every other use of a 32-bit value is a 32-bit instruction, a load or store
of 32 bits, or an argument or return value, whose high half the ABI leaves
undefined.

